    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d115">

                <pb facs="0239" n="183" sameAs="frus:frus1944Quebec/pg183"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">J.C.S. Files</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Representatives of the British
                        Chiefs of Staff</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d115-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/> C.C.S. 618/2</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-08-29">29 August 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Machinery for Coordination of
                        United States-Soviet-British Military Effort</hi></p>
                <p rendition="#flush">References: C.C.S. 618<anchor n="2" xml:id="d115-f2"/> and
                        618/1<anchor n="3" xml:id="d115-f3"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>In C.C.S. 618 we circulated the text of certain messages which had been
                        exchanged between the Head of the British Military Mission in Moscow<anchor
                            n="4" xml:id="d115-f4"/> and the British Chiefs of Staff concerning a
                        proposal put forward by Marshal Stalin to the United States Ambassador in
                            Moscow<anchor n="5" xml:id="d115-f5"/> at the end of June,<anchor n="6"
                            xml:id="d115-f6"/> to the effect that it might be desirable to create
                        some machinery through which the efforts of our respective forces could be
                        coordinated.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The British Chiefs of Staff have since discussed this matter with General
                        Burrows and have instructed us to put forward the attached memorandum which
                        represents their views.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>The British Chiefs of Staff have been informed of the text of C.C.S. 618/1
                        and suggest that the whole matter should be discussed at the next
                        Conference.</item>
                </list>


                <xi:include href="d115-a1.xml" xpointer="d115-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d115-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d115-f1-content">
                <p>For the discussion of this paper by the Combined Chiefs of Staff at their 172d
                    Meeting, September 12, 1944, see <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg307">p. 307</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d115-f2" n="2" xml:id="d115-f2-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg9">p. 9</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d115-f3" n="3" xml:id="d115-f3-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d115-f4" n="4" xml:id="d115-f4-content">
                <p>Lieutenant General Montagu Brocas Burrows.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d115-f5" n="5" xml:id="d115-f5-content">
                <p>W. Averell Harriman.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d115-f6" n="6" xml:id="d115-f6-content">
                <p>According to Deane, <ref target="#pg152">pp. 152</ref>–<ref target="#pg153"
                        >153</ref>, Stalin made this proposal to Harriman on June 28, 1944.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
